= Solution
{parent=a}

A complex matrix is diagonalisable when it is similar to a diagonal matrix, equivalently when the vector space has a basis of its eigenvectors.

See also: <Diagonalizable matrix>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=8a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

If $Av=\lambda v$, then $p(A)v=p(\lambda)v$, proving one inclusion. Conversely, if $\mu$ is an eigenvalue of $p(A)$, factor $p(z)-\mu=c\prod_j(z-\lambda_j)$. If none of the $\lambda_j$ were eigenvalues of $A$, every $A-\lambda_jI$ would be invertible, making $p(A)-\mu I$ invertible, a contradiction. Thus $\mu=p(\lambda)$ for some eigenvalue $\lambda$ of $A$.

See also: <Spectral mapping theorem>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=8a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Write $A=S\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n)S^{-1}$. The power series gives $e^A=S\operatorname{diag}(e^{\lambda_1},\ldots,e^{\lambda_n})S^{-1}$. Therefore
$$\det(e^A)=\prod_i e^{\lambda_i}=e^{\sum_i\lambda_i}=e^{\operatorname{tr}A}.$$

See also: <Matrix exponential>, <Diagonalizable matrix>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= d
{parent=8a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The vectors $(1,0,1)^T,(0,1,0)^T,(1,0,-1)^T$ are eigenvectors of $B$ with eigenvalues $2,1,0$. Applying the exponential to these eigenspaces gives
$$e^B=\begin{pmatrix}(1+e^2)/2&0&(e^2-1)/2\\0&e&0\\(e^2-1)/2&0&(1+e^2)/2\end{pmatrix}.$$

See also: <Matrix exponential>, <Spectral decomposition>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

