= 1F
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Numbers and Sets}

= Solution
{parent=1f}

The series $\sum a_n$ converges when its partial sums tend to a finite real limit. Put $m=n+1$. Then
$$a_n=\frac{2\sqrt m}{\sqrt{m-1}+\sqrt{m+1}}-1>0$$
by strict concavity of the square root. Taylor expansion at $m$ gives
$$\sqrt{m-1}+\sqrt{m+1}=2\sqrt m\left(1-\frac1{8m^2}+O(m^{-4})\right),$$
so $m^2a_n\to1/8$. Limit comparison with $\sum n^{-2}$ proves convergence.

See also: <Convergent series>, <Limit comparison test>, <Taylor theorem>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= 2E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Numbers and Sets}

= a
{parent=2e}
