= 3B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Methods}

= Solution
{parent=3b}

Let $r_1,r_2$ be the roots of $\alpha r^2+\beta r+\gamma=0$. The causal Green function is $G(t,\tau)=H(t-\tau)g(t-\tau)$, where
$$g(s)=\frac{e^{r_1s}-e^{r_2s}}{\alpha(r_1-r_2)}$$
for distinct roots, and $g(s)=se^{rs}/\alpha$ for a repeated root. These formulas enforce $g(0)=0$ and $\alpha g'(0)=1$, hence the required delta jump.

For the oscillator,
$$y(t)=\int_0^t\frac{\sin(\omega(t-\tau))}{\omega}\sin(\lambda\tau)\,d\tau.$$
If $\lambda\ne\omega$, this is
$$y(t)=\frac{\sin(\lambda t)-(\lambda/\omega)\sin(\omega t)}{\omega^2-\lambda^2}.$$
At resonance $\lambda=\omega$, the limiting expression is
$$y(t)=\frac{\sin(\omega t)-\omega t\cos(\omega t)}{2\omega^2}.$$

See also: <Causal Green function>, <Forced harmonic oscillator>, <Resonant forcing>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= 4B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Electromagnetism}

= Solution
{parent=4b}

Maxwell's equations are
$$\nabla\cdot E=\rho/\varepsilon_0,\quad \nabla\cdot B=0,
\quad\nabla\times E=-\partial_tB,
\quad\nabla\times B=\mu_0J+\mu_0\varepsilon_0\partial_tE.$$
Taking the divergence of Ampère–Maxwell and using Gauss's law gives
$$\partial_t\rho+\nabla\cdot J=0.$$
Therefore
$$\dot Q=-\int_{\partial D}J\cdot n\,dS=0$$
because $J$ vanishes on the boundary. Componentwise integration by parts also gives
$$\frac d{dt}\int_Dx\rho\,d^3x=-\int_Dx\nabla\cdot J\,d^3x=\int_DJ\,d^3x,$$
with the boundary term again zero.

See also: <Maxwell equations>, <Continuity equation>, <Electric dipole moment>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= 5D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics}

= Solution
{parent=5d}

For incompressible inviscid flow without body force,
$$\nabla\cdot u=0,qquad \partial_tu+(u\cdot\nabla)u=-\rho^{-1}\nabla p.$$
Taking curl gives
$$\partial_t\omega+(u\cdot\nabla)\omega=(\omega\cdot\nabla)u,qquad \omega=\nabla\times u,$$
equivalently $\partial_t\omega=\nabla\times(u\times\omega)$.

Direct differentiation of the stated field gives $\omega=u$. Hence $u\times\omega=0$, so the vorticity equation gives $\partial_t\omega=0$ and the field is steady. Finally
$$(u\cdot\nabla)u=\nabla(|u|^2/2)-u\times\omega=\nabla(|u|^2/2).$$
Euler's equation therefore implies
$$\nabla\left(p+\frac12\rho|u|^2\right)=0,$$
so $H$ is spatially uniform.

See also: <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid>, <Vorticity equation>, <Beltrami flow>, <Bernoulli function>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

