= Solution
{parent=c}

If $V=V_1\oplus V_2$ were a nontrivial invariant decomposition, each restricted minimal polynomial would be a power of $x-\alpha$ of degree at most $\dim V_i<n$. Their least common multiple is the minimal polynomial of $\phi$, contradicting its degree $n$.

Since $m_\phi$ has degree $n$, $\phi$ has a cyclic vector $b_1$. Put $b_k=\phi^{k-1}b_1$. Then $\phi(b_k)=b_{k+1}$ for $k<n$. In this basis the matrix is the companion matrix with ones immediately below the diagonal and last column
$$c_{k+1}=(-1)^{n-k+1}\binom nk\alpha^{n-k},\qquad0\le k<n,$$
obtained by expanding $(x-\alpha)^n$.

See also: <Invariant direct-sum decomposition>, <Cyclic vector>, <Companion matrix>.

