= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

If a prime divided every coefficient of $fg$, reducing modulo that prime would make the product of the two nonzero reduced primitive polynomials zero in a domain, impossible. Thus a product of primitive polynomials is primitive. Factoring the contents from arbitrary $f,g$ then gives
$$c(fg)=u,c(f)c(g)$$
for a unit $u$.

See also: <Gauss lemma for polynomials>, <Polynomial content>.

