{parent=d}

If $\beta\in\mathcal O_L$, all of its $K$-conjugates are algebraic integers, so their sum and product give
$$\operatorname{Tr}_{L/K}(\beta),\ N_{L/K}(\beta)\in\mathcal O_K.$$

Conversely, if these two quantities lie in $\mathcal O_K$, then $\beta$ is a root of
$$X^2-\operatorname{Tr}_{L/K}(\beta)X+N_{L/K}(\beta),$$
a monic polynomial whose coefficients are algebraic integers. Part (a), or transitivity of integral dependence, implies $\beta\in\mathcal O_L$.

See also: <Trace and norm integrality criterion for a quadratic extension>, <Field trace>, <Field norm>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= e
{parent=20g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Let $\beta=a+b\alpha\in\mathcal O_L$. The nontrivial $K$-automorphism sends $\alpha$ to $-\alpha$, so
$$\operatorname{Tr}_{L/K}(\beta)=2a\in\mathcal O_K.$$
Also $\alpha$ is integral, hence $\alpha\beta$ is integral, and
$$\operatorname{Tr}_{L/K}(\alpha\beta)=2b\alpha^2
=2b(4+\sqrt{17})\in\mathcal O_K.$$
But $4+\sqrt{17}$ has norm $-1$, so it is a unit of $\mathcal O_K$. Multiplying by its inverse proves $2b\in\mathcal O_K$.

See also: <Trace and norm integrality criterion for a quadratic extension>, <Unit from norm one or minus one>.

