= b
{parent=28l}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Conditionally on $X_t=n$, the $n$ arrival times are the order statistics of independent $\operatorname{Unif}[0,t]$ variables. For $n\geq1$, the last jump occurs before $3t/4$ exactly when every point lies before that time, so the probability is
$$\left(\frac34\right)^n.$$
The number falling in $(t/4,3t/4)$ is therefore
$$\operatorname{Binomial}\left(n,\frac12\right).$$

See also: <Poisson process conditional arrival times>, <Order statistic>, <Binomial thinning conditional on a total>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=28l}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A particle arriving at time $s\leq t$ survives until $t$ with probability $e^{-\mu(t-s)}$. Independently retaining each Poisson arrival with this time-dependent probability is Poisson thinning. Hence the number alive at time $t$ is Poisson with mean
$$\lambda\int_0^te^{-\mu(t-s)}ds
=\frac\lambda\mu(1-e^{-\mu t}).$$
Thus
$$N_t^{\rm alive}\sim\operatorname{Poisson}\left(\frac\lambda\mu(1-e^{-\mu t})\right).$$

See also: <Poisson thinning>, <Exponential survival probability>, <Infinite-server queue transient distribution>.

